# The `bsalons` project — the same database the Flutter app and the operator
# console use. This app is a client of it; it owns no schema.
#
# One pair of credentials serves both halves of the merged application: the
# marketing pages read the salon index at BUILD time to prerender it, and the
# waitlist form calls `join_app_waitlist()` from the browser; the product reads
# and writes as its signed-in user through `@supabase/ssr`.
#
# The publishable (anon) key is safe in a browser: Row-Level Security is the
# gate, and every write goes through a SECURITY DEFINER RPC that authorises the
# caller itself. The waitlist is the sharpest illustration — `app_waitlist` has
# RLS on with no policies and no table grants, so this key cannot read one row
# of the list it just joined.
#
# There is no service-role key here and there must never be one. This app acts
# only as its signed-in user — that is the whole security model.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://izlyevebmxqlxinigote.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=sb_publishable_Ve9ENI8gAovucWDHRzKuRg_TaVW_Uhe

# The public origin, used for canonical URLs, `metadataBase`, the sitemap and
# robots. Read through `lib/site.ts`.
#
# `||`, not `??`, in that module: a declared-but-blank value is a common CI
# accident and would otherwise produce `https:///salon/…`.
#
# Inlined at `next build` because it is `NEXT_PUBLIC_`, so changing it needs a
# rebuild rather than a redeploy of the same bundle.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# The operator console (`../admin`), a separate application on its own origin.
# Only the marketing footer's "Admin portal" link would read it.
#
# `NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL` used to live here too and is **gone**: it pointed at
# `tho_web` as a different origin, and the merge made the product these same
# routes. An unset value would have sent a production visitor to their own
# machine. Links into the product are relative now — see `brand.appPaths`.
NEXT_PUBLIC_ADMIN_URL=http://localhost:3002
